# Implementation & Roadmap Doctrine

## Verify Paths and Contracts Against Code

When configuring healthchecks, OpenAPI, or integration points, **verify paths and contracts against the actual code**. Do not assume from PRD, spec, or documentation.

- **Validation error status:** This project maps request body validation (e.g. FastAPI `RequestValidationError`) to **400 Bad Request**, not 422. Integration tests and API docs should expect 400 for validation failures.

- Health endpoint: Grep for the router path (e.g. `/health` vs `/v1/health`) in the API modules
- OpenAPI: The live spec at `/openapi.json` when the app runs is canonical; static specs can drift
- Docker healthcheck: Use the path that exists in the app; prefer stdlib (e.g. `urllib.request`) over external tools (curl) in slim images

## Definition of Done as Scope Boundary

Use the roadmap step's **Definition of Done** as the authoritative completion checklist. Do not add scope beyond it. Each checkbox in the DoD is a deliverable; when all are satisfied, the step is complete. Avoid over-engineering or "helpful" extras that are outside the step.

## User-Stated Design Overrides Spec

When the user **explicitly** states a design preference, mental model, or future intent that differs from the roadmap or PRD (e.g. "remove the menu button," "panel beside the nav," "translates to bottom nav on mobile"), treat the user's statement as the current authority and implement accordingly. Do not refuse on the grounds that the written spec says something different. Document divergence if needed per Design Doc and Implementation Divergence.
- **Optional spec behaviour:** When the user says an optional behaviour is not needed (e.g. "collapse isn't needed, can reopen from nav"), remove or simplify that behaviour rather than retaining it for spec compliance.

## Demo Triggers for Unwired UI

When delivering a **new UI component** whose real trigger will be added in a later step (e.g. a lightbox that will open from a map pin click in a future phase), add a **temporary demo trigger** (e.g. a button or test-only entry point) so the user can verify the component before the full integration exists. Mark the trigger as temporary in code or comments and remove it when the real integration is in place.

## User Intensifiers and Defaults

When the user uses intensifiers ("much thinner," "much smaller," "a lot more"), choose a value that **clearly satisfies the stronger end** of the range rather than a moderate compromise, to avoid repeated iteration.

## Deprecated Integration Cleanup

When removing a **deprecated integration** (e.g. a dependency or feature that is no longer used), **search the codebase** for all references (imports, env vars, config, README) before removing. Remove the dependency and update the lockfile; leave design or roadmap docs (PRD, implementation roadmap) unchanged unless the user explicitly asks to change product direction. This keeps cleanup complete without over-scoping.

## Feature Removal: Cascade Cleanup

When **removing a feature** (UI, overlay, or integration path), find **all** references before removing: state variables, refs, hooks that consume them (e.g. `useFocusTrap(ref, { active: open })`, `useEffect` that closes on Escape), event handlers that update that state, and imports. Remove or update them in one pass. Leaving one reference (e.g. a ref or a hook still bound to the removed state) causes `ReferenceError` or stray behaviour when the code runs. Grep for the feature’s identifiers (state name, ref name, overlay name) and resolve every hit.

## Design Doc and Implementation Divergence

When **implementation diverges from a PRD or roadmap** (e.g. a different provider, technology, or approach than specified—such as OpenFreeMap instead of Protomaps), **document the divergence** so the codebase and docs stay aligned. Either (a) add a short note in README or Design/ stating the current implementation (e.g. "The map uses OpenFreeMap; PRD v5 describes the earlier Protomaps plan"), or (b) update the PRD/roadmap to reflect the chosen approach. Leaving design docs to describe a superseded approach without a pointer to reality misleads future readers and agents.

## Export Script Fallback

When an automated export script (e.g. OpenAPI from FastAPI app) cannot run due to environment constraints (venv, deps, sandbox, missing env vars), **provide a manual fallback** rather than blocking delivery.

- Option A: Manually author the artifact from route definitions and schemas
- Option B: Document that the script requires full environment and how to run it; provide a static reference that is "good enough" for the current need
- Document both the automated path (when env allows) and the manual/fallback path

## Scripts That Import the Application

Scripts that `import app` (or equivalent) trigger full app bootstrap: Settings validation, DB drivers, OAuth config. They require either:

- **(a) Full environment:** venv activated, `.env` present, all deps installed
- **(b) Minimal env stubs:** Set required env vars in the script before any app import

Document which is required. If the script uses stubs, set them at the top before importing. If it requires full env, state that clearly in the script docstring and in README/CONTRIBUTING.

## Service vs Schema Validation in Tests

When the **service layer** enforces a rule that the **request schema** also enforces (e.g. "at least 2 items"), a normal request will fail schema validation (e.g. 400/422) before the service runs. To test that the service correctly rejects the case too (and returns the right error), bypass schema validation for that test: e.g. use Pydantic's `Model.model_construct(...)` to build the payload so the service receives it and its validation runs. This keeps service-level error messages and behavior under test.

## ORM Ordered Relationships After Mutations

After **in-process mutations** to an ordered relationship (e.g. reordering or deleting child rows and flushing in the same session), the parent's relationship collection (e.g. `route.route_photos`) can be **stale**: it may still reflect the pre-mutation order or set. Do not rely on it for "current order." Use an **explicit query** that selects the children with the appropriate `order_by` (and options like `selectinload` if you need related data), and use that result for recomputation or serialization. This applies whenever you need the authoritative order after reorder/delete in the same request or test.

## Integration Tests and Service Invariants

When a **service** enforces invariants (e.g. "route distance must be > 0" or "photos must not all be at the same point"), **integration tests** that hit that code path must supply data that satisfies those invariants. For example, if the service builds a route from two photo locations, use two distinct coordinates (e.g. different lat/lng per photo) so the computed distance is non-zero. Mock or fixture data that violates the invariant will cause the service to raise and the test to fail even when the API contract is correct.

## Database Test Isolation and Shared State

When writing tests that **query the database** (e.g. list/browse endpoints), establish whether the test DB is **shared** and whether other tests commit. In this project, the default `db_session` fixture rolls back only its own transaction; integration tests and other sessions may commit. A test that assumes "only my data exists" (e.g. `assert total == 1`) will fail when run alongside others. Either: **(a)** Assert on the **presence or absence of the specific entities** the test created (e.g. "our record id is in the result" / "our record id is not in the result"), so tests remain valid with shared state; or **(b)** Use isolation (e.g. a fixture that truncates the relevant tables before the test) when exact counts or strict isolation are required. Prefer (a) unless the test explicitly needs exact totals.

## Verification When Tests Cannot Be Run

If the execution environment **cannot run the project's test suite** (e.g. venv not activated, pytest/uv not on PATH), **do not claim that tests pass**. State explicitly that tests could not be executed, give the exact commands the user should run to verify (e.g. `cd backend && pytest tests/unit/... -v -k '...'`), and treat the implementation as unverified until the user confirms. Delivering code without running tests is acceptable when the environment blocks execution; claiming verification without execution is not.

## Geometry and Serialization in Tests

When asserting on GeoJSON coordinates from geometry (e.g. Shapely `mapping(to_shape(geom))` or similar), the serialized coordinates may be **tuples**, not lists. Tests that compare to list literals (e.g. `assert coords[0] == [-122.4, 37.8]`) can fail due to type mismatch. Use `list(geom["coordinates"][i])` in the assertion, or assert tuple equality, so the comparison is consistent with the actual serialized type.

## Conditional Simplification and Null Safety

When you **simplify a conditional** (e.g. change `if (!a || !b)` to `if (!a)`), re-audit the block that runs when the condition is true. No code in that block may use a variable that is now guaranteed to be null or undefined there (e.g. in the `if (!map)` branch, do not call `map.getSource(...)`). Otherwise you introduce a runtime error that static analysis may not catch.
